  "answer_markdown": "## Biggest bottleneck: checkout completion\n\nYour clearest leak is **after shoppers reach checkout**, not a lack of initial traffic.\n\n| Shopify funnel step | Sessions | Step conversion |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Store sessions | 4,452 | — |\n| Added to cart | 112 | **2.52% of sessions** |\n| Reached checkout | 101 | **90.2% of carts** |\n| Completed checkout | 34 | **33.7% of checkouts** |\n\nYou are getting people from cart into checkout unusually well, but roughly **67 of 101 checkout sessions did not complete**. That makes checkout the first commercial bottleneck to investigate. The second is the low **2.52% session-to-cart rate**, likely involving traffic quality, value communication and trust.\n\n### Fix this first: a 48-hour checkout audit\n\nBefore changing ads or pricing:\n\n1. **Confirm the kit is purchasable everywhere.** The public research captured contradictory “Sold out” and “48 kits remaining” messaging. Test homepage → both product URLs → cart → payment on Android and iPhone. Do one live prepaid order and, if offered, one COD order. Verify inventory, ₹299 prepaid savings, shipping, taxes and confirmation messages.\n2. **Inspect Shopify’s abandoned checkouts.** For the 67 non-completers, record the last step reached, payment method attempted, payment error, device/browser and whether shipping or other charges appeared unexpectedly. Do not contact test orders.\n3. **Make local payment routes obvious.** Confirm UPI, cards and COD—if operationally available—work without redirects or errors. Keep guest checkout enabled and remove nonessential fields.\n4. **Put final cost and reassurance before checkout.** Near the CTA and cart, state the actual delivered price, delivery expectation, payment options, prepaid saving and plain-language guarantee eligibility.\n5. **Remove stock contradictions.** Do not use “48 left” or scarcity unless inventory is genuinely connected and accurate.\n\nIf checkout completion moved from 33.7% to **45%**, the same 101 checkout sessions would mathematically produce about **45 completed sessions instead of 34**. That is an illustration, not a forecast.\n\n## Do not scale ads yet\n\nMeta reports 31 attributed purchases from ₹62,996 spend:\n\n- **Reported CAC:** approximately ₹2,032\n- **Reported ROAS:** approximately 1.62\n- Shopify displayed AOV: ₹3,176.87\n- Approximate pre-ad contribution at that AOV: **₹2,113/order** after ₹650 product cost, ₹350 shipping and 2% payment fee\n- Approximate amount remaining after blended Meta CAC: only **₹81/order**, before RTO, reverse shipping, packaging, taxes, overhead or profit\n\nSo blended acquisition is approximately at the provisional first-order ceiling—not comfortably profitable. Meta attribution also does not prove incrementality.\n\nThe strongest campaign is **“Testing Campaign Shopify – Broad”**: 24 attributed purchases at ₹1,693 CAC and 1.95 ROAS. It may have roughly ₹420/order left before currently unknown costs. Preserve it at a controlled budget while diagnosing checkout; do not scale it yet. The other active “New Baby” campaign has only ₹1,807 spend and no reported purchases, which is too little history for a final verdict, but its ₹69 landing-page-view cost warrants a tight cap rather than expansion.\n\n## Second fix: improve product-page commitment\n\nAfter checkout works, address the **2.52% session-to-cart rate**. The public memo found limited visible substantiation and social proof in extracted page text. For a new child-safety brand, replace broad promises with specific, supportable proof:\n\n**Suggested buy-box copy**\n\n> **One kit for six common, reachable hazards in Indian homes**  \n> Edge and corner protection, socket guards, cabinet/fridge locks and door guards—plus 1-to-1 installation video support.  \n> **₹3,299** · Save ₹299 on prepaid orders  \n> 30-day money-back guarantee and eligible-failure replacement terms apply.  \n> **Check compatibility and guarantee details before ordering.**\n\nAdd directly beside this copy: exact quantities, compatibility limits, authentic customer installation photos/reviews with permission, delivery/payment information and links to full guarantee terms. Avoid “entire home,” universal-fit or safety guarantees unless documented.\n\n## Priority order\n\n1. **Purchasability and stock consistency** — emergency check.\n2. **Checkout/payment abandonment** — largest measured leak.\n3. **Product-page trust and bundle-value clarity** — improve add-to-cart rate.\n4. **Then optimise creatives and scale the winning campaign** using post-RTO contribution, not platform ROAS alone.",
